# A Conductive 2D Conjugated Tetrathia[8]circulene‐Based Nickel Metal–Organic Framework for Energy Storage

## abstract
Herein, a novel D4 symmetrical redox‐active ligand tetrathia[8]circulene‐2,3,5,6,8,9,11,12‐octaol (8OH‐TTC) is designed and synthesized, which coordinates with Ni2  ions to construct a 2D conductive metal‐organic framework (2D c‐MOF) named Ni‐TTC. Ni‐TTC exhibits typical semiconducting properties with electrical conductivity up to ≈1.0 S m−1 at 298 K. Furthermore, magnetism measurements show the paramagnetic property of Ni‐TTC with strong antiferromagnetic coupling due to the presence of semiquinone ligand radicals and Ni2  sites. In virtue of its decent electrical conductivity and good redox activity, the gravimetric capacitance of Ni‐TTC is up to 249 F g−1 at a discharge rate of 0.2 A g−1, which demonstrates the potential of tetrathia[8]circulene‐based redox‐active 2D c‐MOFs in energy storage applications.

Herein, a novel ${\pmb D}_{4}$ symmetrical redox-active ligand tetrathia[8]circulene2,3,5,6,8,9,11,12-octaol (8OH-TTC) is designed and synthesized, which coordinates with ${\mathsf{N i}}^{2+}$ ions to construct a 2D conductive metal-organic framework (2D c-MOF) named Ni-TTC. Ni-TTC exhibits typical semiconducting properties with electrical conductivity up to ${\approx}1.05\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ at $29816.$ Furthermore, magnetism measurements show the paramagnetic property of Ni-TTC with strong antiferromagnetic coupling due to the presence of semiquinone ligand radicals and $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ sites. In virtue of its decent electrical conductivity and good redox activity, the gravimetric capacitance of Ni-TTC is up to $249\mathsf{F}\mathsf{g}^{-1}$ at a discharge rate of $\mathbf{0.2Ag^{-1}}$ , which demonstrates the potential of tetrathia[8]circulene-based redox-active 2D c-MOFs in energy storage applications.
## 1. Introduction
In recent years, tetra-hetero[8]circulenes appear to be even more intriguing due to their specific physicochemical properties as unique structural motifs for organic electronic devices[1] and sensors.[2] As a typical class of tetra-hetero[8]circulenes, tetrathia[8]circulenes, eight alternating benzene and thiophene rings form an octagon with novel aromatic features: the outer fused rings are aromatic and the inner eight-membered ring is anti-aromatic, resulting in a highly conjugated planar and $D_{4}$ symmetrical architecture with superior charge transport ability and promising semiconducting performance.[3] Although some theoretical[3b,4] and practical[3a,c,5] studies have investigated the electronic structure and charge transport of tetrathia[8]circulenes, the research on their applications has been largely lagged behind. As a result, designing and synthesizing new materials to further realize the practical application of tetrathia[8]circulenes in electrochemistry represents the forthcoming task for chemists.
Currently, metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) have been the cutting-edge subjects for the research of functional materials due to their unique porosity, high surface area, and chemically adjustable pore dimension, which are widely studied and applied in gas separation[6] and storage.[6,7] In general, most conventional MOFs with poor charge transport ability are regarded as insulators, which greatly limits their applications in the fields of electrocatalysis, energy storage, and conversion where the charge transport process acts as a vital role. The situation has changed significantly with the emerging of 2D conductive MOFs (c-MOFs). They are constructed via planar coordination between various conjugated organic linkers and transition metal nodes with the structural designability and functional tunability, resulting in in-plane $\pi\mathrm{-}\mathrm{d}$ conjugation and the charge delocalization over the meworks.[8] Therefore, these unique characteristics of 2D c-MOFs make them promising candidates for energy storage applications.[9]
Energy storage is a constant and important issue in daily life and technology applications. Electrochemical capacitors (ECs), also known as supercapacitors, have garnered attention because of their high power density, rapid charge-discharge capability, long cycle life, and low maintenance cost.[10] Based on the charge storage mechanism, supercapacitors can be classified into electrical double-layer capacitors (EDLCs) and pseudocapacitors (PCs), which store energy through ion adsorption at the electrode/electrolyte interface and electron transfer by reversible redox reactions, respectively.[11] Interestingly, 2D c-MOFs usually possess large specific surface area and good electrical conductivity, which are beneficial to increase the EDL capacitance.[9d] Moreover, various redox-active subunits embedded in 2D c-MOFs can effectively improve their pseudocapacitance. The above results indicate that 2D c-MOFs are especially suitable for the supercapacitors. Therefore, taking tetrathia[8]circulenes as an organic linker of 2D c-MOFs is a good strategy to make their application in electrochemical energy storage a reality.
Herein, we design and synthesize a novel metal-organic framework based on the redox-active linker tetrathia[8]circulene-2,3,5,6,8,9,11,12-octaol (8OH-TTC) and the nickel centers. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first work on the synthesis of 2D conductive coordination polymers using tetrathia[8] circulene as parent core structure. In this $D_{4}$ symmetrical metal-organic framework, each 8OH-TTC linker coordinates with four $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions to construct a planar 2D c-MOF, named as Ni-TTC. It comes up to our expectations that Ni-TTC displays decent electrical conductivit and superior electrochemical performance in supercapacitors.
## 2. Results and Discussion
## 2.1. Synthesis and Structural Characterization of Ni-TTC
The ligand 8OH-TTC is synthesized first in this work by the demethylation of octamethoxyl-tetrathia[8]circulene $\mathrm{\nabla}^{8\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{O}.}$ TTC) with boron tribromide and followed by hydrolysis. And then, Ni-TTC is synthesized as black powder from the reaction of 8OH-TTC and $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ in a mixed solvent of $0.2\mathrm{mLN}$ , N-dimethylformamide (DMF) and $1.0~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water under a lifted temperature of $85~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ by a sealing tube method (Figure 1a).
The sharp diffraction peaks can be observed in the Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) as shown in Figure  1c, which is strong evidence of the high crystallinity of Ni-TTC. The first five peaks could be indexed to (hk0) of a 2D square lattice with $a=\bar{b}=14.46\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , which is in good agreement with DFT simulated single layer of Ni-TTC with the TTC connected via the coplanar square $\mathrm{NiO}_{4}$ subunits. Furthermore, we construct three possible structure models of Ni-TTC, which are eclipsed (AA) stacking, slipped parallel (AA′) stacking, and staggered (AB) stacking models, respectively (Figure  S1, Supporting Information). A comparison between the experimental PXRD data and simulated patterns (Figure  S2, Supporting Information) shows that slipped AA′ stacking model matched better with the observed one. And further profile fitting converges with a $\mathrm{GoF}=1.40\%$ , $\mathrm{R_{p}}=1.87\%$ , and $\mathrm{R_{wp}}=2.47\%$ , which gives us a monoclinic lattice in $\mathrm{P}2/\mathrm{m}$ space group with the unit cell parameters of $a=13.06\textrm{\AA}$ , $b=\bar{1}4.56\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $c=3.73\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $\beta=97.6^{\circ}$ . The small difference between the experimental and simulated data verifies the stacking pattern of Ni-TTC. In the high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HR-TEM) images (Figure 2a,b; Figure S3, Supporting Information), two series of lattice fringes with the $d$ -space of 14.5 and $3.7\mathring\mathrm{A}$ can be clearly identified, which further confirms the periodic crystalline structure of Ni-TTC. The morphology and composition of Ni-TTC are revealed by the scanning electron microscopy (SEM) with energy dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS). As shown in Figure 2c and Figure S4 (Supporting Information), Ni-TTC appears as nano-rods with length ranging from $500\mathrm{nm}$ to $3\upmu\mathrm{m}$ , and widths of $100{-}500~\mathrm{nm}$ . The spectra of EDS (Figure 2d) clearly show that C, O, S, and Ni elements distribute uniformly on the Ni-TTC.
The elemental contents are further confirmed by the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS). As shown in Figure  3a, the survey spectrum of Ni-TTC shows the presence of only Ni, N, C, S, and O resonance peaks from the material itself and solvent guest molecules. We attribute the N peak to DMF molecules being trapped in the pores of the framework during the reaction and washing process. In the high-resolution Ni $2\mathrm{p}$ XPS spectrum (Figure S5, Supporting Information), one set of peaks $2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}$ and $2\mathrm{p}_{1/2}$ levels of Ni are observed at the binding energies of ${\approx}855.7\$ and ${\approx}873.4~\mathrm{eV},$ , respectively. The above result shows only Ni (II) is present in Ni-TTC, similar to the case of NiTAA-MOF[12] and $\mathrm{Ni}_{3}(\mathrm{HITP})_{2}$ .[13] The detailed elemental composition is quantitatively revealed by elemental analysis (EA) and inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometer (ICP-OES) (Table  S5, Supporting Information). Based on the EA and ICP-OES analysis results, the chemical formula of $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}(\mathrm{C}_{24}\mathrm{O}_{8}\mathrm{S}_{4})\cdot\mathrm{HCON}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\cdot6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ can be estimated for Ni-TTC. Combining with the EDS and XPS data, the absence of counter-cation species could be confirmed, indicating the neutral character of this c-MOF material.
To evaluate the porosity of Ni-TTC, the $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ isotherm at $77~\mathrm{K}$ is measured with the sample activated at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ The $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherm is shown in Figure 3b and the BET surface area of Ni-TTC is calculated to be a small value $13.67~\mathrm{m}^{2}~\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , which can be explained as the filling of solvent molecules owing to the strong interaction between MOF backbone and solvent molecules. The thermal stability of Ni-TTC is investigated by thermogravimetric analysis (TGA). The results (Figure  S6, Supporting Information) reveal that the as-synthesized framework displays a first weight loss ${\approx}100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After the activation of Ni-TTC by heating at $120~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the weight loss curve becomes much gentler. The difference suggests that what is initially lost are solvent molecules such as water trapped in the framework. In addition, the results of the curve show that the backbone of Ni-TTC is thermally stable up to $250~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , and then, the sharply weight drop means a collapse of the framework since the ligand 8OH-TTC is thermal stable up near to $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Furthermore, there is no change in its PXRD pattern after elevating the temperature (Figure S7, Supporting Information), suggesting its excellent thermal stability.
For characterizing the oxidation state of Ni-TTC, the solidstate electron paramagnetic resonance (EPR) spectrum (Figure 3c) is performed. The spectrum exhibits a broad absorbance band in the range of $2500{-}4500\ \mathrm{G}$ with a $g$ value of 2.036, which belongs to high-spin Ni (II) ions.[14] In addition, a symmetrical signal appearing at ${\approx}3360\mathrm{~G~}$ with a $g$ value of 2.004 represents the existence of organic radicals on redox-active ligand.[12] Considering its neutral character, it can be deduced that four electrons have been removed from one ligand molecule and the four catechol units have been converted into semiquinone radicals.
Ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS) is performed to gain further insight into the band structure of Ni-TTC. As shown in the kinetic region (secondary electron cut off, SECO) (Figure  S8, Supporting Information), the work function of Ni-TTC is ${\approx}4.65~\mathrm{eV}.$ Meanwhile, in the valence band region (the HOMO region) (Figure S9, Supporting Information), the onset of valence features peak edge is ${\approx}0.33\ \mathrm{eV},$ demonstrating that the HOMO orbital leading edge of Ni-TTC located at ${\approx}0.33~\mathrm{eV}$ below Fermi level. Furthermore, we have calculated the electronic property of 2D Ni-TTC lattice using first-principles calculations in Vienna Ab initio Simulation Package (VASP), as shown in Figure  S10a (Supporting Information). The band structure of 2D Ni-TTC featuring Dirac cones intersected by a flat band can realize a slightly distorted square Lieb lattice.[15] Meanwhile, the result for 3D slipped AA stacking structure of Ni-TTC is similar to its monolayer electronic properties (Figure S10c, Supporting Information). According to theoretical calculation, Ni-TTC is a semimetal with no band gap. Nevertheless, the experimental result shows semiconductor behavior which seems to be conflicting with the calculated one. It could be ascribed to quantum confinement effects, that is, Ni-TTC with very small characteristic size (such as small nanoparticles) is a semiconductor.[16]
## 2.2. Magnetism of Ni-TTC
In order to further figure out the oxidation state of nickel ions and ligands, we probe the magnetic properties of Ni-TTC by collecting direct current variable-temperature magnetic susceptibility data. The evaluation of $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}$ (the molar magnetic susceptibility per mole $\mathrm{Ni}_{2}\mathrm{TTC}$ ) gives us more insight into its magnetic properties. In an applied field of $1000\mathrm{Oe}$ , the value of $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}$ is positive and drops dramatically as temperature increases (Figure S11, Supporting Information) suggesting the paramagnetic behavior of Ni-TTC.[12] At the same time, the plot of $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}^{}^{-1}$ versus $T$ (Figure  3d) displays a nearly straight line in hightemperature region which is in line with the Curie–Weiss law well. Curie constant of 4.35  emu  mol−1  K and Weiss constant of $-105.26\mathrm{~K~}$ are obtained by linear fitting in the high temperature region (Figure S12, Supporting Information). The negative Weiss temperature indicates the presence of antiferromagnetic coupling in the framework, and the more negative value than other $\mathrm{MOFs^{[17]}}$ indicates the existence of stronger interactions in Ni-TTC. At $300~\mathrm{K},$ the plot of $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T$ versus $T$ (Figure 3e) exhibits a value of $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T=3.21$ emu $\mathrm{mol^{-1}}$ K, which is very close to the value of $\chi_{\mathrm{M}}T=3.50\mathrm{\emu\mol^{-1}\ K}$ theoretical expected for two magnetically isolated $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ -centered of $S=1$ spin and one ligand $(\mathrm{TTC-O^{4\cdots}})$ possessed multiple radicals located on the semiquinone structures of four ${\mathrm{S}}=1/2$ spins.[18] Thus, this result proves that the paramagnetic properties of Ni-TTC are related to both $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ ions and semiquinone ligands.
## 2.3. Electrical Conductivity of Ni-TTC
Electrical conductivity of Ni-TTC is measured by conventional four-contact probe variable-temperature method on compressed samples at the temperature range of 300–405 K. The pelletized samples exhibit typical semiconductor properties and Arrhenius behavior. At the same time, Ni-TTC gives a decent conductivity of ${\approx}1.0\ \mathrm{S\m^{-1}}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ and ${\approx}5.0~\mathrm{S~m^{-1}}$ at $405\mathrm{~K~}$ (Figure 3f ), which is comparable to most of the conductive MOFs ever reported (as shown in Table S6, Supporting Information), and higher than most of the catechol-type ligand-based MOFs. The high conductivity of Ni-TTC is mainly attributed to: 1) the 2D layer structure formed by $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions and conjugated 8OH-TTC linkers offers an effective charge transport pathway via throughbond conduction. 2) $\pi\cdot\pi$ stacking between Ni-TTC layers provides spatial and orbital overlap which enables charge transport by through-space conduction.[19]
## 2.4. Supercapacitor Performance of Ni-TTC
The initial evaluation of the electrochemical performances is conducted by a three-electrode system in 1  M KCl aqueous electrolyte. Cyclic voltammetry (CV) curve (Figure  S13, Supporting Information) is obtained at the scan rate of $1\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , which reveals that the capacitance characteristics of Ni-TTC are governed by both EDL capacitance and faradaic redox reactions since the nearly rectangular curve with three distinct redox peaks at −0.302/−0.379  V, $0.074/-0.068\mathrm{~V~}$ and $0.296/0.104\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs SCE). The presence of the clear redox peaks in the cyclic voltammograms at low scan rates indicates that redox reaction is the nonnegligible charge-storage mechanism in this highly conjugated system. To probe the nature of this faradaic process, CV curve of 8OH-TTC (Figure S14, Supporting Information) is obtained at the same condition. Two pairs of redox peaks appear at $0.083/0.029\mathrm{~V~}$ and 0.298/0.161 V (vs SCE), suggesting the quasi–reversible redox activity of ligand. By comparison of the CV curve of 8OH-TTC and Ni-TTC, it could be concluded that the redox process appears to be metal-centered and ligand-based redox activity, that is, the two redox peaks at $0.074/-0.068\mathrm{~V~}$ and $0.296/0.104\mathrm{~V~}$ (vs SCE) correspond to the redox reaction of ligand and the extra redox peak can be attributed to the valency changes of nickel $(\mathrm{Ni^{II}/N i^{I I I}})$ . As a result, the possible mechanism of reversible redox reaction during charge/discharge process can be concluded in Scheme S3 (Supporting Information).
The gravimetric capacitance $\mathrm{(C_{g})}$ calculated from the CV curve is $245\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at the scan rate of $1\ \mathrm{mV}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ (Figure  4a). Regarding the rate performance, the Ni-TTC retains only $27\%$ of its capacitance (Figure  S15, Supporting Information) on increasing the scan rates from 1 to $100\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ , which can be ascribed that there is not enough time to allow the redox reactions occurring as the increase of scan rates and the EDL becomes the dominant mechanism since the redox peaks become less and less obvious until they disappear at $100\mathrm{mVs^{-1}}$ . To distinguish the EDL capacitance $\mathrm{(C_{dl})}$ and pseudo-capacitance $\d(C_{\mathrm{p}})$ contribution in this process, capacitance differentiation is calculated by Dunn method (Figure 4e,f). The contribution of $\mathrm{C_{\mathrm{p}}}$ is significant at low scan rates, for instance, the contribution of $\mathrm{C_{\mathrm{p}}}$ is close to $80\%$ at the scan rate of $10\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ (Figure S16, Supporting Information). Furthermore, the contribution of $\mathrm{C_{\mathrm{p}}}$ decreases gradually and the dominant contribution of capacitance is replaced by $\mathrm{\DeltaC_{dl}}$ with the increase of scan rates. At last, the contribution of $\mathrm{C_{\mathrm{p}}}$ increases to $70\%$ when the scan rate is up to $100\ \mathrm{mV\s^{-1}}$ (Figure S17, Supporting Information). This result is highly consistent with its poor rate performance.
The galvanostatic charge and discharge (GCD) curves of Ni-TTC electrode (Figure  4b) are approximately symmetric triangular shapes with small plateaus. The $\mathrm{C_{g}}$ calculated from the discharge curve is $249\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at a discharge rate of $0.2\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ which is comparable or even higher than other 2D c-MOFs in
Table S7 (Supporting Information). Moreover, its specific capacitance remains as high as $65\%$ $(249-161\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}})$ when current densities increase 20 times $(0.2\mathrm{-}5\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}})$ (Figure  S18, Supporting Information), which is much higher than the capacitance retention of increasing scan rates. This result further shows that redox reaction is of vital importance in the chargedischarge process of Ni-TTC. Moreover, the capacitive performance of the Ni-TTC sample is well maintained with cycling, in which $80\%$ of the capacitance is retained after 1800 galvanostatic charge/discharge cycles performed at a current density of $2\mathrm{A}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Figure 4c). Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) for the Ni-TTC (Figure  4d) shows typical supercapacitor characteristics, that is, an almost vertical low frequency region in the Nyquist plot with a low ionic resistance, which indicates an excellent accessibility of ions to active sites.[20]
A symmetric two-electrode system is fabricated based on Ni-TTC electrode to further evaluate the potential applications of Ni-TTC (Figure  S19, Supporting Information). First, the CV curves at different voltage windows (Figure  5a) are obtained and it can be observed that these curves are approximately rectangular until the potential was $1.2~\mathrm{V}.$ However, the CV curves appear with plateaus as the potential continued to increase, which means its ineffective charge when the voltage is more than 1.2V. Consequently, the approximately rectangular CV curves (Figure  5b) are collected at the different current densities under the voltage window of $0{\mathrm{-}}1.2\ \mathrm{V}.$ The GCD curves (Figure  5c) exhibit nearly triangular shapes from 0 to $1.2~\mathrm{V},$ and the gravimetric capacitance is $187\mathrm{~F~g^{-1}}$ at a low discharge rate of $0.2\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ , which is higher than other materials in Table S8 (Supporting Information). The energy density attains $9.35~\mathrm{W~h~kg^{-1}}$ at a power density of $0.11\ensuremath{~\mathrm{kW~}\mathrm{kg}^{-1}}$ and an energy density of $5.19\mathrm{~W~h~kg^{-1}}$ at a higher power density of $2.43\ensuremath{\mathrm{~kW~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{kg}}^{-1}$ , revealing its ideal capacitive behavior and desirable fast charging/discharging property for power devices compared with other materials in Table  S9 (Supporting Information), and the Ragone plot is in Figure  S20 (Supporting Information). In addition, Nyquist plot (Figure 5d) is recorded, revealing a low interfacial $\mathrm{R_{ct}}$ . The capacitance of Ni-TTC retains $54\%$ of the initial capacitance after $2000~\mathrm{GCD}$ cycles at the current density of 2  A $\boldsymbol{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ (Figure  5e). More interestingly, red light-emitting diodes (2.0  V) are successfully lit by connecting three cell devices in series (Figure  5f), which is the powerful evidence for the potential applications of Ni-TTC in energy storage devices.
