## 3. Conclusion
In summary, a novel tetrathia[8]circulene-based 2D c-MOF Ni-TTC is synthesized and characterized. This material displays typical semiconducting behavior with decent electrical conductivity of $1.0\mathrm{~S~m^{-1}}$ at room temperature due to its planar $\pi\mathrm{d}$ conjugation and the charge delocalization over the 2D frameworks. Meanwhile, Ni-TTC exhibits paramagnetic behaviors with strong antiferromagnetic coupling on account of the interaction between $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ ions and semiquinone ligand radicals. Furthermore, the application potential of Ni-TTC in supercapacitors is explored. It exhibits a gravimetric capacitance of $249\ensuremath{\mathrm{~F~}}\ensuremath{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ at a discharge rate of $0.2\mathrm{~A~g^{-1}}$ with decent energy density and good cycling stability, which manifests that Ni-TTC is a promising candidate for supercapacitors. In this work, we explore a reasonable path and open a new door for going through the bottleneck period of the application of tetrathia[8] circulenes. The problem of the limited electrochemical appli
